!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/35/ltr8/http/agent-1.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/35/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/35/m/core.tn"
@doc:"""
  TSON Agent AST — the structured middle representation
  (draft 2, brought to 2026 Revision 35 in the meta-service experiment).

  Three representations, as in any modern compiler: the PLAN is the
  user grammar — an ordinary TSON map document, `name => !method
  { request }` entries and a final `return`, references written as
  path tokens (`login.user.id`, `"orders.items[0].name"`), failure
  substitutes as `@or`, the interface as `@interface`. This module
  is the AST the plan reading produces: everything the surface left
  implicit is structural here — paths parsed to segment lists,
  references distinguished from constants by shape, `or` a field
  (it is a required action, so it cannot ride as annotation
  metadata), step order an array, the interface a field. The
  BYTECODE (agent-vm.tn) is what the AST lowers to; memory slots
  are step indices, so the flattening IS the slot assignment.

  Plan-reading rules (surface layer): entries in document order;
  step names MUST be identifiers; `return` reserved and final; a
  string-class token whose head segment names a prior step is a
  path, totally, with rename-not-quote as the collision resolution;
  request completeness, REQUIRED_DEFAULT injection, and positional
  sugar follow constructor application. `[*]` is reserved in the
  path grammar and has no AST form in this revision — additive
  when fan-out lands.

  Partiality, three layers, one severity each: a partial selection
  yields the absent sentinel, which propagates along the selector
  and MUST land where absence is admitted; a failed call with `or`
  substitutes the declared value; a failed call without it aborts
  with agent_error, discriminated from the derived success contract
  by transport status. Plans cannot branch on values.
  """
{
  @doc:"""
    A name in one of the namespaces a plan borrows -- a step, a method, a field. Not `identifier`, which a
    user schema cannot reach, and not `text`, which admits anything: the identifier grammar as a pattern.
    (§8.2's name hygiene does not reach map keys or atom values, so confusable step names are the
    admission verifier's to refuse.)
    """
  name => !text ^ { pattern: "[\\p{L}_][\\p{L}\\p{N}_-]*" }

  @doc:"""
    A constant, of any shape: core's escape hatch. `dynamic` is §7.8's widest scoped instance and the
    successor to what earlier revisions spelled `unknown` -- the value names its own type, from this
    schema's namespace or from a foreign one it names with a nested !!schema, and is validated in full
    against whatever it named. A value naming no type at all is a validation error here, so this is not
    an `any` that goes unchecked. rpc-1.tn's payload slot is the same slot.
    """
  constant => dynamic

  @doc:"""
    Source spelling of a selection, as the surface writes it: a
    string-class token, `step ( .field | [n] | [f = v] )*`. Kept as
    a named atom for surfaces that speak in source terms —
    agent_error reporting, bindings, rename bridges. Enforcement is
    the admission verifier's (Unicode identifiers put a faithful
    I-Regexp facet out of reach); segment-wise `.` to `/` is the
    RFC 6901 bijection, escape-free by identifier hygiene.
    """
  path => !text ^ { min_length: 1 }

  @doc:"""
    One parsed selection step. Exactly one member (REQUIRED field
    group), by kind:
    field  — record field selection; absence-absorbing
    index  — element selection; total where the source floor proves
             the index, else out of bounds yields the absent
             sentinel
    filter — keep elements whose `field` equals `value`; total,
             empty in, empty out; equality over value spaces
    """
  @doc:"A filter's terms: keep elements whose `field` equals `value`."
  filter => {
    field: name
    value: constant
  }

  segment => {
    ( field:  name
    | index:  uint32
    | filter: filter )
  }

  @doc:"""
    A parsed reference: the source step it reads, then the segments
    applied in order. An empty or absent `segments` list references
    the step's whole response.
    """
  selector => {
    step:     name
    segments: [segment]?
  }

  @doc:"""
    One argument position: a constant, a reference, or structure
    above references. Canonical-form rule: a subtree containing no
    selector MUST be a single `value` (constants fold maximally),
    so `record` and `array` appear only on the spine above a
    reference — one spelling per tree, and compilation stays
    canonical. This is the completed typed quotation of value
    construction: constants, holes, and every recursive position —
    finished, per the structure-templates addendum's lesson that a
    quotation typed slot-by-slot must be finished or not begun. The
    plan source is the held form of the same tree; the layers get
    one outcome each.
    """
  arg => {
    ( value:  constant
    | ref:    selector
    | record: {name => arg}
    | array:  [arg] )
  }

  @doc:"""
    One compiled step: apply `method` to the record built from
    `input`. `or`, when present, is the failure substitute — a
    constant that MUST validate against the method's declared
    response type, so downstream typing is unchanged. Structural,
    not an annotation: it is executed, not read.
    """
  step => {
    name:    name
    method:  name
    input:   {name => arg}
    or:      constant?
  }

  @doc:"""
    A complete plan, resolved -- the AST, named as a resolved schema is named against its source: the
    surface is plan SOURCE, this is the plan, and an `agent` (agent-vm-1.tn) is what it compiles to.
    `steps` order is document order, is
    def-before-use order, and is slot assignment for the lowering;
    the step count is the budget. Admission verification is one
    forward pass: resolve `interface` (pin-checked) and its schema
    chain; type each step's input against its method's request
    type; type each selector against its source step's response
    type, partial transitions only where the target admits absence;
    check each `or` against its response type; derive the return
    contract from `return`. The derived contract is known before
    execution.
    """
  plan => {
    interface: uri
    steps:     [step; 1..]
    return:    arg
  }

  @doc:"""
    Abort payload for an unhandled step failure. `step` names the
    failing entry, `at` the source-spelled selection when the
    failure was a partial selection landing where absence is not
    admitted, `call` the method when the call itself failed,
    `detail` the categorized diagnostic.
    """
  agent_error => {
    step:   name?
    at:     path?
    call:   name?
    detail: text?
  }
}
